<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Suspected primary adrenal insufficiency in a 40-year-old novelist with about three months of tiredness, weight loss, darkening skin, nausea and back ache, a postural fall in blood pressure, low sodium, high potassium and raised eosinophils. Please confirm or exclude the diagnosis, start replacement if confirmed, and plan the search for the cause. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within three days; she is stable today and has been told to go to the emergency department at once for vomiting, fainting, confusion or severe abdominal pain. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does she have primary adrenal insufficiency, and which tests confirm it?</item><item>Should replacement start at once, and at what doses of hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone?</item><item>How should the cause be established, and when are adrenal imaging and tuberculosis testing needed?</item><item>Should lymphoma be kept in the differential, given her weight loss and evening feverishness, and how should it be excluded?</item><item>What sick-day rules, emergency supplies and follow-up do you advise, including screening for associated autoimmune disease?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Austen, a 40-year-old novelist, is seen at the request of Dr. Pemberton-Okafor three days after her first visit. Her written notes trace a gradual decline since the winter: tiredness that is worst by afternoon, loss of appetite with morning nausea, a few episodes of vomiting, about 5 kg of weight loss, a dull ache in the low back by evening, salt craving, lightheadedness on standing, and darkening of her face and hands that friends have remarked on. She has felt feverish on some evenings without drenching sweats. She has had no cough, breathlessness, itching, or lumps. She takes no medicines and has never had corticosteroids by any route. Her periods are regular. No family history of adrenal, thyroid or autoimmune disease is known to her. She has been salting her food since her first visit and vomited once two days ago. She wants a clear explanation and to be able to manage the condition herself. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Jane Austen was born at the Rectory in Steventon, a village in north-east Hampshire, on 16 December 1775, the seventh child and second daughter of the rector, the Revd George Austen, and his wife Cassandra (née Leigh). [D]</item><item>Steventon Rectory was her home for the first 25 years of her life; after some years in Bath and Southampton she moved in 1809 with her mother and sister to a cottage at Chawton, on one of her brother Edward&apos;s Hampshire estates, where she revised her early novels and wrote Mansfield Park, Emma and Persuasion. [D]</item><item>Her only sister, Cassandra, like Jane herself, never married. [D]</item><item>In 1783, as a young child at school in Southampton with her sister, she caught a putrid fever and was very ill and nearly died. [D]</item><item>The chronology in the 1913 family biography dates the first break in her health to about March 1816. [D]</item><item>Her father died on 21 January 1805 after an illness of only forty-eight hours. [D]</item></list><paragraph>14 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

