    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Start hydrocortisone today: 10 mg on waking and 5 mg at 2 pm (15 mg a day), adjusted by weight, energy, appetite, pigmentation and blood pressure rather than by cortisol levels. [S]</item><item>Start fludrocortisone 0.1 mg each morning; check sodium and potassium at two weeks and renin with lying and standing blood pressure at about six weeks, aiming for renin in the upper normal range; she should salt food to taste. [S]</item><item>21-hydroxylase antibodies at the two-week visit (they are not affected by treatment); adrenal CT and tuberculosis testing only if they are negative. [S]</item><item>Keep lymphoma in the differential until weight and symptoms have recovered: weight, fevers, night sweats, lymph nodes and spleen at each visit, and CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis if weight loss, fevers or nodes persist once replacement is established. [S]</item><item>Sick-day rules: double the hydrocortisone for fever above 38 °C and triple it above 39 °C until a day after the fever settles; inject 100 mg into the thigh and call 911 for vomiting, inability to take tablets, or feeling faint. For surgery under general anesthesia or a major illness in hospital, hydrocortisone 100 mg intravenously at induction and then 200 mg a day until she is eating, tapering back over one to two days. [S]</item><item>An emergency hydrocortisone injection kit with training today, a steroid emergency card, a medical ID, and a written emergency letter. [S]</item><item>Screen for associated autoimmune disease: TSH (normal today) at least yearly; A1c and celiac serology with total IgA at the two-week visit, and vitamin B12 (normal at intake) yearly; ask about periods at each visit and check FSH if they become irregular or stop. [S]</item><item>Repeat the blood count at two and six weeks; no further anemia work-up unless it fails to recover. [S]</item><item>Endocrinology review at least yearly. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Pemberton-Okafor, who sees her the same day, and the first doses were taken in clinic. The diagnosis, the reason for lifelong replacement and the sick-day rules were explained to Ms. Austen, who asked careful questions, read through the written rules, and gave a practice injection on a trainer. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Endocrinology review in one year, or sooner for an adrenal crisis, negative antibodies, or symptoms that do not settle; Dr. Pemberton-Okafor continues prescribing and monitoring and will share the two- and six-week results. [S]</item></list></text>

